    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "218fdbbe_a5f456ed",
        "filename": "ui/build/sandbox_darwin.go",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 61,
      "author": {
        "id": 1074628
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-05-04T02:28:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The way exec works is that you give it a path name and a list of arguments (which becomes argv in the next program). The path name needs to be either an absolute or relative path, but argv[0] is usually the name that the user specified in order to run the command.\n\nThe normal exec.Command() will copy the path to the first argument, then if it isn\u0027t a relative or absolute path, lookup the program with exec.LookPath. For most of our commands, we\u0027re using a relative path, so c.Path and c.Args[0] will be identical, but for \"make\", the path with be something like \"/usr/bin/make\" and the first argument will still be \"make\".\n\nSince we\u0027re wrapping the command line, we only need one name for the executable (sandbox-exec will do the same expansion). I choose to copy Path to Args[0], since that\u0027s guaranteed to produce the correct binary, where Args[0] could have been rewritten to something completely different.",
      "parentUuid": "29e4f233_5e5cbb79",
